> if (is_human(ptr)) adjalign(-sgn(u.ualign.type));
>   if (is_demon(ptr)) adjalign(sgn(u.ualign.type));

This is brokenly indented. Each of those two ifs only controls the one
statement following; that is, just the one adjalign() call. For
clarity's sake, it should have been indented like this:

   if (is_human(ptr)) adjalign(-sgn(u.ualign.type));
   if (is_demon(ptr)) adjalign(sgn(u.ualign.type));


>   if(!(ptr->geno & G_GENO)) {

This is the only if that matters. It controls everything contained in
the following {} block...

>    if(flags.soundok) {
>  /* fixme: unconditional "caverns" will be silly in some circumstances */
>        if(flags.verbose)
>    pline("A thunderous voice booms through the caverns:");
>        verbalize("No, mortal!  That will not be done.");
>    }

(BTW, that intermediate if only determines whether you get a message or
not, not whether something happens.)

>    continue;

...that is, up to here at least. There should be a closing brace just
after this. The continue statement jumps to the next iteration of the
enclosing loop statement; i.e., the current case will not be finished.
But this is controlled only by whether the monster type pointed at by
ptr has the G_GENO flag set or not, _not_ by the two preceding ifs.

